From 99c0fd793e59e68c259dfa25e1430ccdc2a77c5a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Knifethrower <132020881+Knifethrower@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 13:43:15 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] core: free the libraries of movies loaded by AVM1 once
 nothing uses them

Every loaded SWF gets a MovieLibrary (characters, bitmaps and their GPU
textures, tessellated shapes, the SWF data). The registry only held its key
weakly, but the library held its own movie strongly, so no library was ever
dropped: memory grew with every loadMovie until the process exited
(ruffle-rs/ruffle#24864). Games that load a SWF per scene ran out of memory
within minutes on 1 GB handhelds.

A library is now a garbage collected object (MovieLibraryGc). The registry
holds it weakly, and display objects keep the library of their movie alive
(DisplayObjectBase::library, set when an object gets a parent and when a movie
is loaded into a clip). Libraries are pinned (held for the life of the
player, as before) unless the movie was loaded by AVM1 (loadMovie,
MovieClipLoader, images included) or is a data-less placeholder movie (unloaded
clips, loading and error stand-ins). The root movie and all AVM2 content stay
pinned. RUFFLE_FREE_LOADED_MOVIES=0 keeps every library, as upstream does.

RUFFLE_MEMLOG: compressed library bitmaps and parsed shape records are tagged
instead of counted as static, so they leave the Library category when freed.

Machinarium (AVM1, a SWF per scene and per hint book page), hint book opened
and closed 20 times in one scene, the series plus this patch: on a PC (Xvfb,
llvmpipe) RSS 262 -> 265 MB, with RUFFLE_FREE_LOADED_MOVIES=0 297 -> 347 MB;
on an RG Cube XX (Knulli, Mali-G31, aarch64 build of the same tree) 276 to
298 MB over the 20 cycles with no trend. Ruffle regression tests avm1/,
avm2/, import_assets/, fonts/, mixed_avm/ (1962): all pass with and without
this patch.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
